# PII: these files are important for reverse engineering but contain real patient data
# (MRN, DOB, physician name). Matched by BASENAME, not by path, deliberately —
# these were pinned to scrapers/myChart/clo-to-jpg-converter/, a directory that
# has since been renamed twice, so the guard silently stopped matching anything
# while still reading as if it protected something. A slightly broad PII rule is
# the right failure direction; re-pull the files under any name and they stay out.
**/viewer.html
**/amf_metadata.bin
*.clo
# Allow synthetic (non-PII) CLO test images for fake-mychart
!fake-mychart/src/data/clo-images/*.clo

# Downloaded MyChart logos (large, stored in S3)
scrapers/list-all-mycharts/logos/
.next/

# Vite build output for the OpenRecord splash demo
openrecord-splash/dist/

# Coverage reports written by `bun run test:coverage`.
# Anchored: a bare `coverage` would also swallow tests/coverage/, the gate's own
# test directory, and silently keep it out of the repo.
/coverage

# TLS keys/certs, env files with secrets, and TS build caches must never be
# committed from anywhere in the tree. Basename rules on purpose: five PRs in
# a row picked up a stray web/ directory carrying a localhost key and a Sentry
# auth token, each fixed by re-cutting the branch — which cleans the symptom
# and leaves the files on disk for the next branch. `.env` alone (line above)
# misses variants like `.env.sentry-build-plugin`.
*.pem
.env.*
*.tsbuildinfo
